           SECTION 1: TWO WORDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="two-words">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Two Words, One Question</h2>

        <p>Two adjectives get attached to Christian belief so often that they have
        nearly stopped meaning anything: <strong>apostolic</strong> and
        <strong>confessional</strong>. They are not synonyms, and the relationship
        between them is the whole subject of this study.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Apostolic</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">ἀποστολικός</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">Belonging to, deriving from, or agreeing
          with the apostles. Applied to belief, it asks a historical question: is this
          what the men Christ commissioned actually taught? Applied to ministry, it
          asks a different question: does this office stand in continuity with theirs?
          The two questions come apart, and much of the division between traditions
          turns on which one is doing the work.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Confessional</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">ὁμολογία</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">From <em>homologia</em> — literally "saying
          the same thing." A confession is a public, corporate, written statement of
          what a church believes Scripture teaches, to which its teachers are held.
          Applied to a church, "confessional" means the church has committed itself to
          a text and can be measured against it.</span>
        </div>

        <p>So <em>apostolic</em> names the source and <em>confessional</em> names the
        method. The apostolic deposit is what was handed over; a confession is the
        church's attempt to say it back accurately, in words that a particular
        generation's errors made necessary. Nobody confesses in a vacuum. Every
        historic confession is shaped by the thing it was written against, which is
        why reading one without knowing its controversy is like reading half of a
        phone call.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Why This Matters to a Modern Believer</h3>

        <p>The modern objection to confessions is well known and worth taking
        seriously: <em>no creed but the Bible</em>. It sounds like humility. In
        practice it rarely delivers what it promises, for three reasons.</p>

        <p>First, the slogan is itself a creed — a one-sentence doctrinal statement
        about the sufficiency and perspicuity of Scripture, asserted without argument.
        Second, no one comes to Scripture without an interpretive framework; the only
        choice is between a framework that is written down where others can test it
        and one that is invisible even to the person holding it. Third, the historic
        confessions are the most heavily stress-tested summaries of Scripture in
        existence. Ignoring them does not make a reader more biblical; it makes a
        reader more alone.</p>

        <p>The opposite error is just as real. A confession can quietly become the
        thing that is actually believed, with Scripture demoted to a proof-text quarry
        that supplies footnotes for conclusions already reached. Every confessional
        tradition has produced people who could recite the catechism and had stopped
        reading the Bible. The confessions themselves usually warn against this more
        sharply than their critics do.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Key Principle</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A confession is a <em>norma normata</em> — a
          normed norm. Scripture is the <em>norma normans</em> — the norming norm. A
          confession has real authority, derived and answerable; the moment it stops
          being correctable by Scripture it has stopped doing its job. Nearly every
          major confession says this about itself, in its own words, on its first
          page.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: NT CREEDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="nt-creeds">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Creeds Inside the New Testament</h2>

        <p>The first Christian creed was not written at Nicaea. It was written inside
        the New Testament, and in several places the New Testament writers tell us
        they are quoting something older than the letter they are writing.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Handed Over, Received</h3>

        <p>The clearest case is Paul's summary of the gospel to the Corinthians, where
        he frames it with the technical vocabulary of formal transmission:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For I delivered to you as of first importance what I also received: that
          Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, that he was
          buried, that he was raised on the third day in accordance with the
          Scriptures, and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:3-5">1 Corinthians 15:3–5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Paredōka / parelabon</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">παρέδωκα / παρέλαβον</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">"I handed over" and "I received" — the paired
          technical terms for the careful transmission of a fixed body of teaching.
          Paul uses them again of the Lord's Supper tradition
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23">1 Corinthians 11:23</a>).
          The vocabulary is deliberate: Paul is not summarizing his own thought, he is
          passing on something he was given.</span>
        </div>

        <p>The significance is hard to overstate. Writing in the mid-50s, Paul says he
        <em>received</em> this formula — which places its composition earlier still,
        plausibly within a few years of the crucifixion. Whatever one concludes about
        later creeds, the existence of a fixed, transmitted, four-clause summary of the
        gospel is not a fourth-century development. It is apostolic practice from the
        beginning.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Other Fragments</h3>

        <p>Once the pattern is visible, it appears throughout the New Testament — in
        rhythmic, compact, self-contained units that interrupt the surrounding prose:</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 6:4">Dt 6:4</a></span><span><strong>The Shema</strong> — "Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one." The confession Israel already had, and the one every later creed is answerable to.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 10:9">Rom 10:9</a></span><span><strong>The minimum confession</strong> — "if you confess with your mouth that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved." Two clauses: a person and an event.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 8:6">1 Cor 8:6</a></span><span><strong>The Shema rewritten around Jesus</strong> — "one God, the Father... and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things." Paul splits the Shema's own words and places Christ inside the identity of the one God.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:6-11">Php 2:6–11</a></span><span><strong>The Christ hymn</strong> — pre-existence, self-emptying, incarnation, obedience to death, exaltation, universal confession that "Jesus Christ is Lord."</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:15-20">Col 1:15–20</a></span><span><strong>The image of the invisible God</strong> — Christ as agent of creation, head of the church, firstborn from the dead, the one in whom all fullness dwells.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:16">1 Tim 3:16</a></span><span><strong>Six clauses, introduced as common property</strong> — "Great indeed, we confess, is the mystery of godliness: He was manifested in the flesh, vindicated by the Spirit, seen by angels, proclaimed among the nations, believed on in the world, taken up in glory."</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:8">2 Tim 2:8</a></span><span><strong>A two-clause reminder</strong> — "Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, the offspring of David." Resurrection and Davidic descent, the same pair as <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 1:3-4">Romans 1:3–4</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:4-6">Eph 4:4–6</a></span><span><strong>The sevenfold unity</strong> — "one body and one Spirit... one Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God and Father of all." A confession built for the purpose of holding a church together.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:1-2">Heb 6:1–2</a></span><span><strong>A catechism outline</strong> — repentance, faith toward God, instruction about washings, laying on of hands, resurrection of the dead, eternal judgment. Named as the "elementary doctrine," i.e. what converts were already taught.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date"><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Mt 28:19</a></span><span><strong>The baptismal formula</strong> — one name, three persons. This single line does more to shape the structure of every later creed than any other text.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Why the Creeds Are Trinity-Shaped</span>
          The Apostles' and Nicene Creeds are both built in three articles — Father,
          Son, Spirit — because they grew out of the questions put to a candidate at
          baptism, and baptism was administered in the threefold name of
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a>. The creeds are
          not abstract theology that later got attached to the liturgy. They are the
          liturgy, written out.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Deposit Language</h3>

        <p>The New Testament also gives us the category of a fixed body of teaching to
        be guarded. Paul tells Timothy to "follow the pattern of the sound words that
        you have heard from me" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:13">2 Timothy 1:13</a>) and to
        "guard the good deposit entrusted to you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:14">1:14</a>);
        the word is <em>parathēkē</em>, a deposit left with a trustee. Jude writes of
        "the faith once for all delivered to the saints"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a>) — a completed handover, using the
        same <em>paradosis</em> root. Paul tells the Thessalonians to "stand firm and
        hold to the traditions that you were taught by us, either by our spoken word
        or by our letter" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a>).</p>

        <p>That last verse is contested territory, and it is worth being honest about
        why. Catholic and Orthodox readers take it as proof that an oral apostolic
        tradition runs alongside Scripture with equal authority. Protestant readers
        answer that the oral tradition in view is apostolic <em>preaching</em>, which
        is the same content later inscripturated, and that Paul is not authorising a
        second, unverifiable stream. Both readings are serious. Neither is settled by
        the verse alone — which is exactly why the question needed confessions.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Key Principle</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Summarising the faith in fixed, transmissible
          form is not a departure from apostolic Christianity. It is an apostolic
          practice, visible in the New Testament's own pages, undertaken for the same
          reasons the later church undertook it: teaching converts, unifying scattered
          congregations, and marking off the boundary where the gospel has been
          exchanged for something else.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: RULE OF FAITH
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="rule-of-faith">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Rule of Faith</h2>

        <p>Between the apostles and the first ecumenical council lies a period of
        roughly two centuries in which there was no New Testament canon list anyone
        would recognise, no empire-wide council, and no mechanism to convene one. What
        the churches of that period did have was something they called the
        <em>rule of faith</em>.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Regula fidei / kanōn tēs pisteōs</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">κανὼν τῆς πίστεως</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The rule, or measuring-line, of faith — a
          short summary of the apostolic message used to catechise converts, to shape
          baptismal questions, and to test whether a teacher's reading of Scripture
          stayed inside apostolic bounds. Not itself a fixed text; a fixed
          <em>content</em> that different writers phrase differently.</span>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Three Independent Witnesses</h3>

        <p>The rule surfaces in three major writers, in three different regions, within
        about fifty years of each other — and they agree.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Irenaeus of Lyons, c. AD 180</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><cite>Against Heresies</cite> I.10.1 — the faith of a church scattered across the world.</div>
          <p>Irenaeus states that the church, "though dispersed throughout the whole
          world, even to the ends of the earth, has received from the apostles and
          their disciples this faith" — and then gives it: one God the Father Almighty,
          maker of heaven and earth and sea and all things in them; one Christ Jesus,
          the Son of God, incarnate for our salvation; the Holy Spirit, who proclaimed
          through the prophets the dispensations of God; the birth from a virgin, the
          passion, the resurrection, the bodily ascension, and the future coming to
          judge. He is writing in Gaul, in Greek, as a native of Asia Minor who had
          heard Polycarp, the disciple of John.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Tertullian of Carthage, c. AD 200</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><cite>Prescription Against Heretics</cite> 13 — the same content, in Latin, in North Africa.</div>
          <p>Tertullian gives a rule in almost identical sequence: one God, creator of
          the universe, who produced all things out of nothing through his Word; that
          Word called his Son, seen by the patriarchs, born of the virgin Mary, crucified
          under Pontius Pilate, raised the third day, taken into heaven, seated at the
          Father's right hand, who will come to judge the living and the dead and who
          sent the Holy Spirit. He calls this rule "taught by Christ" and insists it
          "raises no other questions."</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Origen of Alexandria, c. AD 225</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><cite>On First Principles</cite>, preface — the rule as a boundary marker for speculation.</div>
          <p>Origen, the most speculative theologian of the pre-Nicene church, opens his
          most speculative work by setting out the apostolic preaching as a fixed
          boundary, and distinguishing between what the apostles delivered plainly
          (God, Christ, the Spirit, the soul, free will, the devil, the world's
          creation and end, the inspiration of Scripture) and what they left open for
          inquiry. It is a striking piece of evidence: even the era's boldest mind
          treats the rule as given, not negotiable.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">What This Evidence Does and Does Not Prove</span>
          Three writers, in Gaul, North Africa, and Egypt, writing in two languages,
          without councils or a settled canon, independently summarise the faith in the
          same sequence with the same content. That is strong evidence that the
          apostolic message had a recognisable, transmissible shape well before any
          council defined it. It is <em>not</em> evidence that later dogmatic
          development — of any tradition — was contained in that rule. The rule is
          short. Much of what divides the churches today is not in it, on any side.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Old Roman Symbol</h3>

        <p>Out of the baptismal interrogation at Rome came a short, stable formula
        modern scholars call the Old Roman Symbol, attested by the middle of the second
        century and recognisable as the direct ancestor of the Apostles' Creed. The
        legend that each of the twelve apostles contributed one clause is reported by
        Rufinus around AD 404; it is a pious story rather than history, and the received
        Latin text of the creed as we now say it is not attested in full until the
        eighth century. None of this makes the creed less apostolic in
        <em>content</em>. It does mean the name describes what it teaches, not who
        typed it.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: ECUMENICAL CREEDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="ecumenical">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Ecumenical Creeds</h2>

        <p>Four documents carry the title "ecumenical" — universal — in the sense that
        they were intended for the whole church rather than one region or party. They
        are the common inheritance, and they are the reason the overlap in the next
        section is as large as it is.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381)</h3>

        <p>The crisis was christological. Arius, a presbyter of Alexandria, taught that
        the Son was the first and highest of created beings — genuinely exalted, but on
        the creature side of the line between God and everything else. The slogan was
        blunt: "there was when he was not." The council's answer was equally blunt, and
        it turned on a single non-biblical word.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Homoousios</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">ὁμοούσιος</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">Of the same substance, or of one being. The
          word does not appear in Scripture, and that was the objection at the time.
          The council's defence was that every biblical word available had already been
          successfully re-defined by the Arians; an extra-biblical term was needed to
          protect a biblical claim. This is the classic instance of a confession using
          new words to say an old thing.</span>
        </div>

        <p>The creed used liturgically today is not the 325 text but the expanded form
        associated with the Council of Constantinople in 381, which greatly enlarged
        the third article to confess the Holy Spirit as "the Lord, the giver of life,
        who proceeds from the Father, who with the Father and the Son together is
        worshipped and glorified." It was ratified at Chalcedon in 451 and is properly
        the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Filioque — One Word, Two Churches</span>
          The Western church later added <em>filioque</em>, "and the Son," to the
          procession clause: the Spirit proceeds from the Father <em>and the Son</em>.
          It appears at the Third Council of Toledo in 589 as a local Spanish measure
          against residual Arianism, spread through the Frankish realms under
          Charlemagne, and was resisted at Rome for two centuries before being adopted
          there in the early eleventh century. The East objected on two grounds: the
          theological one, that it compromises the Father's role as sole source within
          the Trinity; and the procedural one, that no local church may unilaterally
          amend an ecumenical creed. It remains the single clause of the Nicene Creed
          that the Eastern churches do not confess, and it is a standing item in every
          East–West dialogue.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Chalcedon (451)</h3>

        <p>Nicaea settled that the Son is fully God. The next century asked how the
        fully divine Son is related to the genuinely human Jesus. Chalcedon's answer
        works mostly by negation — it fences the mystery rather than explaining it:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          One and the same Christ, Son, Lord, Only-begotten, acknowledged in two
          natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without
          separation; the distinction of natures being in no way annulled by the union,
          but rather the characteristics of each nature being preserved and coming
          together to form one person and subsistence.
          <cite>The Definition of Chalcedon, 451</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The four adverbs are the load-bearing part. Two of them rule out collapsing
        the natures into a hybrid; two rule out splitting Christ into two acting
        subjects. Chalcedon is also where the creeds' method becomes clearest: it
        secures a biblical confession by marking off what cannot be said, leaving the
        positive mystery intact.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Athanasian Creed</h3>

        <p>The <em>Quicunque vult</em> is Western, Latin, and almost certainly dates
        from around AD 500 — which means Athanasius, who died in 373, did not write it.
        It is the most technically precise of the four, working through the Trinity and
        the incarnation in tight parallel clauses, and it is the only one with explicit
        damnatory clauses attached. It is received in the Lutheran and Anglican
        confessional collections and is not used in the East.</p>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Application</span>
          <p>If you want one practical test of whether a teaching is inside historic
          Christianity, the Nicene Creed is still the best short instrument available.
          It is ancient enough to predate every current division, short enough to read
          in ninety seconds, and specific enough that the movements which have departed
          from orthodox Christianity nearly always have to modify it. Learn it well
          enough to notice when something is missing.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

