        <h3 class="tg-subsection">One Genuinely Surprising Convergence</h3>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Joint Declaration, 1999</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">On the doctrine that split the West, Lutherans and Catholics signed a common statement.</div>
          <p>On 31 October 1999, in Augsburg, the Lutheran World Federation and the
          Catholic Church signed the <cite>Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of
          Justification</cite>. Its central paragraph confesses "that by grace alone,
          in faith in Christ's saving work and not because of any merit on our part, we
          are accepted by God and receive the Holy Spirit, who renews our hearts while
          equipping and calling us to good works." The document then states that the
          sixteenth-century condemnations, on this specific point as here articulated,
          no longer apply to the partner. The World Methodist Council associated itself
          in 2006, the Anglican Consultative Council in 2016, and the World Communion
          of Reformed Churches in 2017.</p>
          <p>What it does not do is dissolve the disagreement. The Declaration is
          carefully scoped: it addresses a basic common understanding, and it lists the
          remaining differences in language, theological elaboration, and emphasis
          rather than pretending they are gone. Imputation versus transformation,
          the role of merit, and the certainty of salvation are all still live.
          But a modern believer who has been told that Catholics teach salvation by
          works and Lutherans teach a fiction of righteousness should read the actual
          document before repeating either claim.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Where the Overlap Genuinely Breaks</h3>

        <p>Honest mapping means naming the exceptions rather than rounding them away:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The Assyrian Church of the East</strong> did not receive Ephesus
          (431), and the <strong>Oriental Orthodox</strong> — Coptic, Syriac, Armenian,
          Ethiopian, Malankara — did not receive Chalcedon (451), holding instead to
          one incarnate nature of the Word. Sustained dialogue since the 1970s has
          produced agreed statements concluding that the historic difference is
          substantially one of terminology rather than of the faith confessed. That
          conclusion is accepted by many on all sides and not by everyone.</li>
          <li><strong>The filioque</strong> is the one clause of the Nicene Creed the
          Eastern churches do not say.</li>
          <li><strong>The Second Council of Nicaea (787)</strong>, on the veneration of
          images, is received as ecumenical by Catholics and Orthodox and generally not
          by Protestants — which is why some Protestants count four ecumenical councils
          and some count seven.</li>
          <li><strong>Oneness Pentecostals</strong> reject the distinction of persons in
          the Godhead, holding a modalist account. This is a real departure from the
          Nicene confession, and it is worth noting that the Assemblies of God adopted
          its Statement of Fundamental Truths in 1916 largely to answer this very
          controversy from within Pentecostalism.</li>
          <li><strong>Some modern denominations</strong> have made creedal subscription
          optional or advisory, so that the official confession no longer describes what
          is taught in many of their pulpits. The document on the website and the
          doctrine in the building are not always the same thing.</li>
          <li><strong>Groups outside the Trinitarian confession</strong> altogether —
          Unitarian, Latter-day Saint, Jehovah's Witness — are outside this shared
          core by their own account as much as by anyone else's.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Key Principle</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The shared core is larger in volume than
          everything that divides the traditions. It feels smaller because the
          disagreements are concentrated at the point where the gospel is
          <em>applied</em> to an individual — how grace reaches a person, who may
          receive the sacraments, what assurance is available. Those questions are
          pastorally hot, which is why a small remaining area of dispute occupies most
          of the oxygen.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: DEVELOPMENT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="development">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How the Traditions Diverged</h2>

        <p>No confession was written in the abstract. Each is a response to a specific
        crisis, and knowing the crisis explains the emphasis — including the places
        where a confession is strangely long about something that now seems minor, or
        conspicuously silent about something that now seems central.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Shape of the Whole</h3>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">30–100</span><span><strong>Apostolic kerygma.</strong> Transmitted formulas, baptismal confession in the threefold name, the "pattern of sound words."</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">100–325</span><span><strong>The rule of faith.</strong> Local baptismal creeds; Irenaeus, Tertullian, Origen; no canon list, no councils, remarkable agreement.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">325–787</span><span><strong>The conciliar era.</strong> Nicaea 325, Constantinople 381, Ephesus 431, Chalcedon 451, Constantinople II 553, Constantinople III 680–81, Nicaea II 787.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">431 · 451</span><span><strong>The first partings.</strong> The Church of the East after Ephesus; the Oriental Orthodox after Chalcedon.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">529</span><span><strong>Orange.</strong> Semi-Pelagianism condemned; even the beginning of faith confessed as a gift. The canons were then largely lost to the medieval West and recovered in the sixteenth century.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1054</span><span><strong>East and West.</strong> Mutual excommunications formalise a separation long in the making — filioque, papal jurisdiction, and much that was cultural and political.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1517–1580</span><span><strong>The confessional age.</strong> Within sixty years the Western church produces more binding doctrinal texts than in the previous millennium.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1545–1563</span><span><strong>Trent.</strong> Rome answers the Reformation point by point and in doing so becomes, for the first time, confessional in the modern sense.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1600s</span><span><strong>Consolidation and division.</strong> Dort, Westminster, Savoy, the Baptist confessions, Dordrecht; the Synod of Jerusalem answers a Calvinising patriarch in the East.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1730s–1784</span><span><strong>Revival and Methodism.</strong> Doctrine carried by preaching, hymns and societies as much as by articles.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1870 · 1962–65</span><span><strong>Vatican I and II.</strong> Papal infallibility defined; then a substantial re-framing of revelation, the church, and other Christians.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1906–1916</span><span><strong>Pentecostalism.</strong> Azusa Street; the Assemblies of God Statement of Fundamental Truths written partly to settle an internal Trinitarian crisis.</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1974–2000</span><span><strong>Trans-denominational statements.</strong> Lausanne, Chicago, revised Baptist Faith and Message — confessions written for movements rather than for territorial churches.</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Lutheran — the Confession That Tried Not to Divide</h3>

        <p>The <cite>Augsburg Confession</cite> (1530) is often read as the founding
        document of Protestant separation. Its own posture is the opposite. Melanchthon
        wrote it for presentation to Charles V at an imperial diet, and its structure is
        an argument for continuity: Articles I–XXI set out the faith and repeatedly
        claim to teach nothing contrary to the catholic church, while Articles XXII–XXVIII
        are headed as abuses corrected. It is a plea to be recognised as catholic, not a
        declaration of independence.</p>

        <p>Intra-Lutheran conflict after Luther's death — over original sin, the will,
        the Supper, and whether good works are necessary — produced the
        <cite>Formula of Concord</cite> (1577), and in 1580 the whole corpus was gathered
        into the <cite>Book of Concord</cite>: the three ecumenical creeds, Augsburg, its
        Apology, the Smalcald Articles, Luther's two catechisms, and the Formula. That
        ordering is itself a doctrinal statement. The creeds come first.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Reformed — a Family of Confessions, Not One</h3>

        <p>The Reformed tradition confessed early, often, and regionally: the First and
        Second Helvetic Confessions in Switzerland (1536, 1566), the Gallican (1559),
        the Scots Confession (1560), the Belgic Confession (1561), and the
        <cite>Heidelberg Catechism</cite> (1563), which remains the warmest of all the
        confessional documents — its first question asks not what you believe but what
        your only comfort in life and death is, and answers "that I am not my own, but
        belong, body and soul, in life and in death, to my faithful Saviour Jesus
        Christ."</p>

        <p>Two later documents set the tradition's boundaries. The
        <cite>Canons of Dort</cite> (1618–19) were convened to answer the Remonstrance
        of 1610, in which followers of Jacobus Arminius proposed five articles
        qualifying the Reformed doctrine of grace. Dort answered in five heads of
        doctrine, point for point. The mnemonic TULIP is an English coinage from the
        early twentieth century, not a term from the synod, and it flattens the Canons
        badly — they are pastoral, careful, and more interested in comfort than in
        symmetry.</p>

        <p>The <cite>Westminster Confession</cite> (1646) with its Larger and Shorter
        Catechisms (1647) was produced by an assembly called by the English Parliament
        during the civil war. It is the most architecturally complete of the
        confessions, and it became the template for two others: the Congregationalist
        <cite>Savoy Declaration</cite> (1658) and the Particular Baptist
        <cite>Second London Confession</cite> (1677, republished 1689).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Detail Worth Knowing</span>
          The Second London Baptist Confession is deliberately, and largely verbatim,
          Westminster — with the chapters on baptism and the church rewritten. The
          Baptists who wrote it wanted to demonstrate that they differed from their
          Presbyterian and Congregational neighbours at two specific points and agreed
          everywhere else. Reading the 1689 against the 1646 with the differences marked
          is one of the most efficient exercises in historical theology available, because
          the disagreement is isolated on the page.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Anglican — Articles, and a Prayer Book</h3>

        <p>The <cite>Thirty-Nine Articles</cite> (1563, finalised 1571) are shorter and
        looser than the continental confessions, and deliberately so: they were written
        to make one national church habitable by people with genuinely different
        convictions. Article XVII on predestination is the clearest case, worded so that
        a Calvinist and a non-Calvinist can both subscribe in good conscience.</p>

        <p>Anglicanism also confesses through worship. The <cite>Book of Common
        Prayer</cite> (1549, 1552, definitively 1662) shapes belief by shaping prayer —
        the old principle that the law of prayer establishes the law of belief. The
        Chicago-Lambeth Quadrilateral (1886, 1888) later distilled the essentials for
        reunion purposes to four: the Scriptures, the two creeds, the two dominical
        sacraments, and the historic episcopate.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Anabaptist — Confession as Separation</h3>

        <p>The <cite>Schleitheim Articles</cite> (1527) are the earliest Anabaptist
        confession and unlike any other document here. Their seven articles cover
        believers' baptism, the ban, the breaking of bread, separation from the world,
        pastors, the sword, and the oath — and more than half are about the church's
        relation to the surrounding society rather than about God, Christ, or salvation.
        The <cite>Dordrecht Confession</cite> (1632) gave the Mennonite tradition a
        fuller eighteen-article statement. The emphasis on a visible, voluntary, separated
        church is the tradition's defining contribution, and one that Baptists,
        Pentecostals, and most modern free churches inherited without always knowing
        where it came from.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Catholic — Trent, and Two Vaticans</h3>

        <p>The Council of Trent (1545–63) was Rome's answer to the Reformation, and it
        answered in the Reformers' own genre. Session IV (1546) settled the canon and
        received Scripture and unwritten apostolic traditions "with equal affection of
        piety and reverence." Session VI (1547) issued the decree on justification —
        sixteen chapters of positive teaching followed by thirty-three canons of
        anathema, and it is the single most important document for understanding the
        Catholic–Protestant divide.</p>

        <p>The First Vatican Council (1870) defined papal primacy and infallibility. The
        Second (1962–65) re-framed much: <cite>Dei Verbum</cite> on revelation,
        <cite>Lumen Gentium</cite> on the church. A line from <cite>Dei Verbum</cite> 10
        is regularly missed by Protestant critics and is worth quoting exactly: the
        teaching office "is not above the word of God, but serves it, teaching only what
        has been handed on." The <cite>Catechism of the Catholic Church</cite> (1992,
        revised 1997) is the current comprehensive reference.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Orthodox — Why There Is No Eastern Westminster</h3>

        <p>The Orthodox churches have no document occupying the place Trent holds for
        Catholics or Westminster for Presbyterians, and this is a positional claim
        rather than an accident. The faith is held to be sufficiently defined by the
        seven ecumenical councils and carried in the liturgy, so that a new confession
        would imply the deposit had been incomplete.</p>

        <p>The exceptions prove the rule. When a confession attributed to Cyril Lucaris,
        Patriarch of Constantinople, appeared in 1629 with plainly Calvinist content,
        the Synod of Jerusalem under Dositheus answered in 1672 with a point-by-point
        <cite>Confession</cite>. It is respected, frequently cited, and carefully not
        treated as a peer of the councils. Metropolitan Philaret's
        <cite>Longer Catechism</cite> (1830) has a similar standing in the Russian
        church.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Methodist — Doctrine Carried by Preaching</h3>

        <p>When John Wesley provided for American Methodists in 1784, he did not write a
        new confession; he abridged the Thirty-Nine Articles to twenty-four, cutting the
        predestinarian and specifically English material. The <cite>Articles of
        Religion</cite> are therefore Anglican articles with the Calvinism trimmed. But
        Methodist doctrinal standards have never been the Articles alone: Wesley's
        <cite>Standard Sermons</cite> and his <cite>Explanatory Notes upon the New
        Testament</cite> carry equal or greater weight, and much of the tradition's
        distinctive theology — prevenient grace, assurance, Christian perfection — is
        found there rather than in the Articles. The hymns of Charles Wesley did more
        catechising than either.</p>

        <p>The framework often called the Wesleyan Quadrilateral — Scripture, tradition,
        reason, experience — is a twentieth-century name coined by Albert Outler for
        Wesley's actual method. Outler later regretted the term, because it invited the
        misreading that the four are equal partners. In Wesley they are not: Scripture
        rules, and the other three serve it.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Pentecostal — A Confession Written in a Hurry</h3>

        <p>Pentecostalism began as a movement suspicious of creeds, on the grounds that
        a living experience of the Spirit needed no paper. That position lasted about a
        decade. The "New Issue" controversy of 1913–16, in which a substantial body of
        ministers adopted a modalist, Jesus-only account of God and rebaptised
        accordingly, forced the question. The Assemblies of God adopted its
        <cite>Statement of Fundamental Truths</cite> in 1916, and its unusual length on
        the Trinity — far longer than any comparable article in an older confession — is
        the fingerprint of that crisis.</p>

        <p>Its distinctive articles are the seventh and eighth: baptism in the Holy
        Spirit as an experience distinct from and subsequent to conversion, with
        speaking in other tongues as the initial physical evidence
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:4">Acts 2:4</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 10:44-46">Acts 10:44–46</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 19:6">Acts 19:6</a>). Wesleyan-Holiness
        Pentecostal bodies additionally retain a second definite work of sanctification,
        inherited directly from the holiness movement and through it from Wesley.</p>

        <div class="tg-example">
          <span class="tg-example__label">The Pattern</span>
          <p>Read the list again and a rule emerges: <strong>confessions get written
          when a church is forced to say what it means.</strong> Nicaea had Arius.
          Chalcedon had Eutyches and Nestorius. Augsburg had an emperor demanding an
          account. Dort had the Remonstrants. Westminster had a civil war. Trent had
          Luther. The Assemblies of God had the New Issue. A confession's shape is
          always the negative image of the error it was written to exclude — which is
          why a confession is never a complete theology, and was never meant to be.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: FLASHPOINTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="flashpoints">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Where the Confessions Actually Divide</h2>

        <p>Strip away the polemics and the genuine divisions reduce to seven loci. They
        are not independent — where a tradition lands on authority largely predicts
        where it lands on the rest — but they are separable enough to examine one at a
        time.</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Authority</strong> — what norms the church's teaching, and who
          decides when readings conflict.</li>
          <li><strong>Justification</strong> — whether righteousness is reckoned to the
          believer or worked within, and what faith contributes.</li>
          <li><strong>Baptism</strong> — who is a proper subject, and what the rite
          effects.</li>
          <li><strong>The Lord's Supper</strong> — in what sense Christ is present, and
          how he is received.</li>
          <li><strong>Grace, election, and perseverance</strong> — whether grace can be
          finally resisted or lost.</li>
          <li><strong>Church and ministry</strong> — where governing authority sits, and
          how ministers are made.</li>
          <li><strong>Last things</strong> — the state of the dead, and the shape of the
          end.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Root Disagreement</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Six of the seven are downstream of the first.</div>
          <p>If Scripture is the sole final norm, then any tradition — including one of
          great antiquity — may in principle be judged and corrected by it, and the
          burden of proof falls on the practice. If Scripture and Tradition are one
          deposit with a living interpreter, then a doctrine attested in the church's
          continuous life carries authority even where its scriptural basis is
          inferential, and the burden of proof falls on the innovator. Purgatory,
          Marian dogma, the invocation of saints, and the sacramental system all follow
          the same logic from that first choice. This is why arguments that begin over
          a practice usually end over authority, and why the participants often feel
          they are talking past each other. They are — until that question is reached.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">One Division That Is Not Confessional</span>
          Millennial views cut <em>across</em> the confessional families rather than
          along them. Westminster and the Second London are broadly amillennial in
          tone; dispensational premillennialism spread widely among Baptists and
          Pentecostals only after the Scofield Reference Bible of 1909; the Assemblies
          of God statement is explicitly premillennial. Two congregations of the same
          denomination, subscribing the same confession, can differ here — which makes
          this a useful worked example of a third-order doctrine.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: USING CONFESSIONS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="using">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Reading a Confession Rightly</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Because, or Insofar As</h3>

        <p>When a minister subscribes a confession, there are two historic ways to do
        it, and the difference has split denominations.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Quia subscription</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">I subscribe this confession <em>because</em>
          it agrees with Scripture. The confession's content is affirmed as true; a
          discovered error would be grounds for amending the confession through the
          church's process, not for private reservation.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Quatenus subscription</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">I subscribe this confession <em>insofar
          as</em> it agrees with Scripture. Honest in intent, but it can reduce to
          subscribing nothing in particular, since the subscriber remains the judge of
          the "insofar." Most confessional bodies have found that some middle form —
          subscribing the system of doctrine, with stated exceptions recorded and
          judged by the church — is what works in practice.</span>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Doctrinal Triage</h3>

        <p>A modern believer needs a way to hold firm convictions without treating every
        conviction as a test of fellowship. The most useful available framework sorts
        doctrines into three orders.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Order</th>
              <th>Test</th>
              <th>Examples</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>First</strong></td>
              <td>Denial places a person outside the Christian faith. This is the
              territory of the ecumenical creeds.</td>
              <td>The Trinity; the deity and true humanity of Christ; the bodily
              resurrection; justification by grace through Christ's work; the
              authority of Scripture</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Second</strong></td>
              <td>Christians who differ cannot easily form one congregation, but each
              recognises the other as a Christian.</td>
              <td>Baptism's proper subjects; church government; the manner of Christ's
              presence in the Supper; the extent and resistibility of grace; women in
              ordained office</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Third</strong></td>
              <td>Christians may differ within the same congregation without strain.</td>
              <td>Millennial views; the days of creation; disputed matters of
              conscience of the kind <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:1-12">Romans 14:1–12</a>
              addresses</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The framework in this form is associated with R. Albert Mohler Jr., who
        proposed it in 2005, but the instinct is much older and the biblical warrant is
        plain. Paul treats the resurrection as first-order —
        "if Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:17">1 Corinthians 15:17</a>) — and
        food and calendar observance as third-order, where "each one should be fully
        convinced in his own mind" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:5">Romans 14:5</a>).
        The errors are symmetrical: treating a third-order matter as first-order makes
        a sect, and treating a first-order matter as third-order dissolves the faith
        into a mood.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Four Practical Tests</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Read the document, not the summary of it.</strong> Nearly every
          popular account of what another tradition believes is a summary of a summary
          of a polemic. The primary texts are short, public, and mostly free; Trent
          Session VI is about twenty pages, and it says something different from what
          most Protestants have been told it says.</li>
          <li><strong>Find the controversy.</strong> An article that seems
          disproportionate is usually answering something. Ask what, and the article
          becomes intelligible.</li>
          <li><strong>Check the proof texts.</strong> Most confessions cite Scripture
          in the margin. Reading those texts in context, and asking whether they
          actually bear the weight placed on them, is the single most valuable thing a
          believer can do with a confession — and it is precisely what the confessions
          themselves invite. That is the Berean method
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a>) applied to the church's
          own summaries.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask what it costs.</strong> Every position on every flashpoint
          has a text it must work hard to accommodate. A tradition that has no
          difficulties is one whose difficulties have been hidden from you. Knowing
          the cost of your own position is a mark of having actually thought about
          it.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Application</span>
          <p>Learn one confession well — ideally your own church's, read straight
          through with its Scripture citations open — and know the ecumenical creeds by
          heart. That combination gives you both depth and breadth: a detailed account
          of why your tradition says what it says, and a shared vocabulary in which you
          can recognise as a brother or sister someone who differs from you on
          everything downstream of Nicaea. The believer who has done this is
          simultaneously harder to move and easier to live with.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: THE TABLES
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="tables">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Confessional Belief by Tradition</h2>

        <p>What follows is the reference apparatus: for each of the seven flashpoints,
        every major tradition's position, the confessional document that states it, and
        the scriptural evidence that position principally rests on. Quotations are from
        the confessional texts themselves. Each position is given as its own tradition
        would state it.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">How to Read These Tables</span>
          The middle column is documentary evidence: what the tradition has formally
          committed itself to, with the article cited so it can be checked. The right
          column is the scriptural case the tradition itself makes — not an outside
          assessment of whether the case succeeds. Where a tradition is internally
          divided, that is stated rather than smoothed over.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">1 · Authority</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tradition</th>
              <th>Position and confessional evidence</th>
              <th>Scripture appealed to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture and Tradition form one deposit, authentically interpreted by
              the Magisterium. Trent IV receives Scripture and unwritten traditions
              "with equal affection of piety." <cite>Dei Verbum</cite> 10: the three
              "are so linked and joined together that one cannot stand without the
              others" — and the teaching office "is not above the word of God, but
              serves it."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:15">1 Timothy 3:15</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18-19">Matthew 16:18–19</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 16:13">John 16:13</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture within Holy Tradition; the seven ecumenical councils and the
              consensus of the Fathers; no single infallible bishop. Dositheus (1672),
              Decree 2: Scripture is to be interpreted by the church, not by private
              judgement.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 16:13">John 16:13</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:20">2 Peter 1:20</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:28">Acts 15:28</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture alone is the norm; creeds are received as faithful witnesses
              to it. <cite>Formula of Concord</cite>, Epitome, Summary: the prophetic
              and apostolic writings are "the only rule and norm according to which all
              doctrines and all teachers alike must be appraised and judged," while the
              creeds are held "not because they are the judge of Scripture."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:16-17">2 Timothy 3:16–17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8">Galatians 1:8</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed / Presbyterian</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture is sufficient and self-authenticating; councils may err.
              Westminster 1.10: "The supreme judge, by which all controversies of
              religion are to be determined... can be no other but the Holy Spirit
              speaking in the Scripture." Westminster 31.4: all synods and councils
              "may err, and many have erred."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:16-17">2 Timothy 3:16–17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 8:20">Isaiah 8:20</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 19:7-11">Psalm 19:7–11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Anglican</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture is supreme; the church is a witness and keeper of Holy Writ
              but may not ordain anything contrary to it. Article VI: "Holy Scripture
              containeth all things necessary to salvation." Article XX and Article XXI:
              general councils "may err, and sometimes have erred."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:15-17">2 Timothy 3:15–17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 5:39">John 5:39</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8-9">Galatians 1:8–9</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Baptist</strong></td>
              <td>Scripture is the supreme standard; no creed binds the conscience above
              it. Second London 1.10 follows Westminster verbatim.
              <cite>Baptist Faith and Message</cite> (2000) I: Scripture is "the supreme
