            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">2 · Justification</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tradition</th>
              <th>Position and confessional evidence</th>
              <th>Scripture appealed to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>Justification is both the remission of sins and the sanctification and
              renewal of the inner person; righteousness is infused, and increases
              through works done in grace. Trent VI ch. 7 and ch. 10; canon 9 anathematises
              "faith alone" where it excludes cooperation. Trent VI ch. 8 nonetheless calls
              faith "the beginning of human salvation, the foundation and root of all
              justification."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:5">Romans 5:5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:24">James 2:24</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:6">Galatians 5:6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:2">1 Corinthians 13:2</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>Forensic justification is not the organising category; salvation is
              framed as <em>theosis</em>, participation in the divine life, begun in
              baptism and worked out through synergy with grace. Dositheus, Decree 13:
              justified by faith working through love.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:4">2 Peter 1:4</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 17:21-23">John 17:21–23</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:6">Galatians 5:6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:9">1 Corinthians 3:9</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>Justification is God's forensic declaration, received by faith alone,
              on account of Christ. <cite>Augsburg</cite> IV: people "cannot be justified
              before God by their own strength, merits, or works, but are freely
              justified for Christ's sake through faith." The <cite>Apology</cite> IV
              calls this the article on which the church stands or falls.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:21-28">Romans 3:21–28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:5">Romans 4:5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:16">Galatians 2:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:8-9">Ephesians 2:8–9</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed / Presbyterian</strong></td>
              <td>Justification is by the imputation of Christ's obedience and
              satisfaction, received by faith alone. Westminster 11.1: God justifies
              "not by infusing righteousness into them, but by pardoning their sins,
              and by accounting and accepting their persons as righteous... by imputing
              the obedience and satisfaction of Christ unto them." 11.2: faith is "the
              alone instrument of justification; yet is it not alone in the person
              justified."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:1-8">Romans 4:1–8</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 5:21">2 Corinthians 5:21</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 3:9">Philippians 3:9</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:19">Romans 5:19</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Anglican</strong></td>
              <td>Article XI: "We are accounted righteous before God, only for the merit
              of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ by faith, and not for our own works
              or deservings." Article XII: good works follow justification as its fruit
              and cannot put away sin.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:24-28">Romans 3:24–28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:8-10">Ephesians 2:8–10</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 3:5-8">Titus 3:5–8</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Baptist</strong></td>
              <td>Second London 11 reproduces Westminster 11 nearly verbatim, including
