              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:27-29">1 Corinthians 11:27–29</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>A real and objective change, but the manner is confessed as a mystery
              rather than defined scholastically. Dositheus, Decree 17, does use the
              Greek equivalent of transubstantiation; Orthodox theology has generally
              been reluctant to press the term's Aristotelian machinery.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:53-56">John 6:53–56</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:26-28">Matthew 26:26–28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16">1 Corinthians 10:16</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>Sacramental union — the true body and blood are present "in, with, and
              under" the bread and wine, received by all communicants.
              <cite>Augsburg</cite> X: "the body and blood of Christ are truly present
              and distributed to those who eat in the Lord's Supper."
              <cite>Formula of Concord</cite> VII rejects both transubstantiation and a
              merely symbolic reading.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:26-28">Matthew 26:26–28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16">1 Corinthians 10:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:27-29">1 Corinthians 11:27–29</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed / Presbyterian</strong></td>
              <td>A true spiritual presence, received by faith; the elements are not
              changed. Westminster 29.7: worthy receivers "really and indeed, yet not
              carnally and corporally, but spiritually, receive and feed upon Christ
              crucified." 29.6 rejects transubstantiation as "repugnant, not to
              Scripture alone, but even to common sense." Heidelberg Q78–Q79;
              Belgic 35.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16">1 Corinthians 10:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23-26">1 Corinthians 11:23–26</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:63">John 6:63</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 3:21">Acts 3:21</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Anglican</strong></td>
              <td>Article XXVIII: "The Body of Christ is given, taken, and eaten in the
              Supper, only after an heavenly and spiritual manner. And the mean whereby
              the Body of Christ is received and eaten in the Supper is Faith."
              Transubstantiation "cannot be proved by holy Writ; but is repugnant to the
              plain words of Scripture."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16">1 Corinthians 10:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23-29">1 Corinthians 11:23–29</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:53-58">John 6:53–58</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Baptist</strong></td>
              <td>Divided, and the divergence is documented. Second London 30.7 keeps
              Reformed language — worthy receivers "spiritually receive and feed upon
              Christ crucified." <cite>Baptist Faith and Message</cite> VII is
              memorialist: the Supper is "a symbolic act of obedience whereby members...
              memorialize the death of the Redeemer and anticipate His second
              coming."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23-26">1 Corinthians 11:23–26</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:19">Luke 22:19</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:26-29">Matthew 26:26–29</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Methodist / Wesleyan</strong></td>
              <td><cite>Articles of Religion</cite> XVIII follows Anglican XXVIII:
              spiritual reception by faith, transubstantiation rejected. Wesley's
              <cite>Hymns on the Lord's Supper</cite> (1745) press real-presence
              language warmly, and his sermon on "The Duty of Constant Communion"
              urges frequent reception.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16">1 Corinthians 10:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23-29">1 Corinthians 11:23–29</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:19-20">Luke 22:19–20</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pentecostal</strong></td>
              <td><cite>Statement of Fundamental Truths</cite> 6: the Supper is a
              memorial of Christ's suffering and death, a sharing in the divine nature,
              and a prophecy of his return.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:26">1 Corinthians 11:26</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:4">2 Peter 1:4</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:19-20">Luke 22:19–20</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">5 · Grace, Election, and Perseverance</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tradition</th>
              <th>Position and confessional evidence</th>
              <th>Scripture appealed to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>Prevenient grace precedes and enables every good act (Trent VI ch. 5),
              but grace is genuinely cooperative and can be lost through mortal sin and
              restored through penance (Trent VI ch. 14–15). Trent VI ch. 9 and canon 16
              deny that anyone can know with the certainty of faith that they are among
              the predestined, absent special revelation.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:12-13">Philippians 2:12–13</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 9:27">1 Corinthians 9:27</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-6">Hebrews 6:4–6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:4">Galatians 5:4</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>Synergy: grace is always prior, and the human will genuinely
              cooperates. Dositheus, Decree 3, rejects predestination to evil and any
              notion that God wills any to perish; election is understood in terms of
              divine foreknowledge.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:9">1 Corinthians 3:9</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:4">1 Timothy 2:4</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 3:9">2 Peter 3:9</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:20">Revelation 3:20</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>Single predestination: election is unto salvation only.
              <cite>Formula of Concord</cite> XI teaches that God earnestly wills all
              to be saved, that the elect are chosen in Christ apart from foreseen
              merit, and that the lost perish through their own resistance — declining
              to resolve the resulting tension. Faith can be lost; the remedy is
              repentance, and assurance is directed to Christ and the sacraments rather
              than to introspection.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:4-6">Ephesians 1:4–6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:4">1 Timothy 2:4</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 33:11">Ezekiel 33:11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 23:37">Matthew 23:37</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed / Presbyterian</strong></td>
              <td>Unconditional election, effectual grace, and the perseverance of the
              saints. Westminster 3.5: the elect are chosen "out of his mere free grace
              and love, without any foresight of faith or good works." Westminster 17.1:
              those effectually called "can neither totally nor finally fall away."
              The <cite>Canons of Dort</cite> answer the Remonstrance in five heads.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:4-5">Ephesians 1:4–5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:11">Ephesians 1:11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:29-30">Romans 8:29–30</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 9:11-18">Romans 9:11–18</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:28-29">John 10:28–29</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:6">Philippians 1:6</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Anglican</strong></td>
              <td>Article XVII affirms that "Predestination to Life is the everlasting
              purpose of God" and describes its comfort to the godly — while saying
              nothing about reprobation and closing with a caution about handling the
              doctrine. The wording is deliberately capable of a Calvinist and a
              non-Calvinist reading.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:4-5">Ephesians 1:4–5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:29-30">Romans 8:29–30</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:10">2 Peter 1:10</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Baptist</strong></td>
              <td>Internally divided by design. Particular and Reformed Baptists follow
              Westminster (Second London 3 and 17). General and Free Will Baptists hold
              conditional election. <cite>Baptist Faith and Message</cite> V is a
              deliberate compromise: election is "the gracious purpose of God" and "is
              consistent with the free agency of man," and "all true believers endure
              to the end."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:28-30">Romans 8:28–30</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:37">John 6:37</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:28-29">John 10:28–29</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:19">1 John 2:19</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Methodist / Wesleyan</strong></td>
              <td>Prevenient grace is given to all, genuinely enabling and genuinely
              resistible; election is conditional; apostasy is possible.
              <cite>Articles of Religion</cite> VIII: no one can turn to faith "without
              the grace of God by Christ preventing us." Wesley's sermons
              <cite>Free Grace</cite> (1739) and <cite>Predestination Calmly
              Considered</cite> (1752) argue the case directly.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:9">John 1:9</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 2:11">Titus 2:11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:4">1 Timothy 2:4</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-6">Hebrews 6:4–6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 2:20-22">2 Peter 2:20–22</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pentecostal</strong></td>
              <td>Broadly Arminian. The <cite>Statement of Fundamental Truths</cite>
              contains no article on eternal security, an omission that is deliberate
              and leaves the question to the conscience of member churches within an
              Arminian frame.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 3:16">John 3:16</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 2:11">Titus 2:11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:12-14">Hebrews 3:12–14</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:5">Revelation 3:5</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">6 · Church and Ministry</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tradition</th>
              <th>Position and confessional evidence</th>
              <th>Scripture appealed to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>Episcopal government in apostolic succession, with universal papal
              jurisdiction. <cite>Lumen Gentium</cite> 8: the church of Christ
              "subsists in" the Catholic Church; LG 22–23 on episcopal collegiality
              under the Pope as head. Vatican I (<cite>Pastor Aeternus</cite>, 1870)
              defines papal primacy and infallibility.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18-19">Matthew 16:18–19</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:15-17">John 21:15–17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:32">Luke 22:32</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:1-29">Acts 15:1–29</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>Conciliar episcopacy across autocephalous churches; the see of Rome is
              accorded a primacy of honour but not universal jurisdiction. Authority
              rests with councils received by the whole church.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:6">Acts 15:6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:28">Acts 15:28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:18">Matthew 18:18</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1-4">1 Peter 5:1–4</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>The church is defined by gospel and sacrament, not by polity.
              <cite>Augsburg</cite> VII: the church is "the assembly of all believers
              among whom the Gospel is purely preached and the holy sacraments are
              administered according to the Gospel," and for true unity it is enough to
              agree on these. <cite>Augsburg</cite> XIV requires a regular call to
              public ministry. Polity is therefore adiaphora — episcopal in
              Scandinavia, synodical in North America.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:4-6">Ephesians 4:4–6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 10:17">Romans 10:17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19-20">Matthew 28:19–20</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed / Presbyterian</strong></td>
              <td>Rule by elders in graded assemblies — session, presbytery, synod,
              general assembly. Westminster 25 distinguishes the visible and invisible
              church; the <cite>Form of Presbyterial Church Government</cite> (1645)
              sets out the offices; Belgic 30–32 names the marks of a true church.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:1-29">Acts 15:1–29</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">Acts 20:28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 4:14">1 Timothy 4:14</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5">Titus 1:5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Anglican</strong></td>
              <td>The historic episcopate is retained. The Preface to the Ordinal
              (1550, 1662) states that "from the Apostles' time there have been these
              Orders of Ministers in Christ's Church: Bishops, Priests, and Deacons."
              The Chicago-Lambeth Quadrilateral names it the fourth essential for
              reunion.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:23">Acts 14:23</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:1-13">1 Timothy 3:1–13</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5-9">Titus 1:5–9</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Baptist</strong></td>
              <td>Congregational autonomy under Christ, with voluntary association
              between churches. Second London 26.7–26.8 vests power in the local
              congregation. <cite>Baptist Faith and Message</cite> VI: a church is "an
              autonomous local congregation of baptized believers," and "its two
              scriptural offices are that of pastor/elder/overseer and deacon."</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:17">Matthew 18:17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5:4-5">1 Corinthians 5:4–5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 6:3">Acts 6:3</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:1">Philippians 1:1</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Methodist / Wesleyan</strong></td>
              <td>Connexional government. In episcopal Methodism, bishops exercise
              superintendency as an office rather than a third order — Wesley's own
              position when he set apart Thomas Coke in 1784, a step that broke with
              Anglican order and which Wesley defended from the interchangeability of
              elder and overseer in the New Testament.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">Acts 20:28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5">Titus 1:5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:7">Titus 1:7</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pentecostal</strong></td>
              <td>Varied; the Assemblies of God is a cooperative fellowship of
              self-governing churches with a general presbytery.
              <cite>Statement of Fundamental Truths</cite> 11 names a divinely called
              and scripturally ordained ministry, and the gifts of
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11">Ephesians 4:11</a> are held to
              continue.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11-13">Ephesians 4:11–13</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:28">1 Corinthians 12:28</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 16:15-20">Mark 16:15–20</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">7 · Last Things</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tradition</th>
              <th>Position and confessional evidence</th>
              <th>Scripture appealed to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>Particular judgement at death; purgatory as a state of purification
              for those who die in grace but imperfectly purified (Trent XXV;
              <cite>Catechism</cite> 1030–1032); the suffrages of the living assist the
              dead; then general resurrection and the beatific vision.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:13-15">1 Corinthians 3:13–15</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 12:32">Matthew 12:32</a>;
              2 Maccabees 12:45 (deuterocanonical)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Eastern Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>An intermediate state with prayer for the dead, but the Latin doctrine
              of purgatorial satisfaction and cleansing fire is explicitly rejected —
              Dositheus, Decree 18.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 16:22-26">Luke 16:22–26</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 6:9-11">Revelation 6:9–11</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:16-18">2 Timothy 1:16–18</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Protestant traditions generally</strong></td>
              <td>Purgatory is rejected across the board. Article XXII: "The Romish
              Doctrine concerning Purgatory... is a fond thing vainly invented, and
              grounded upon no warranty of Scripture." Westminster 32.1: souls go
              immediately to heaven or hell, and "besides these two places for souls
              separated from their bodies, the Scripture acknowledgeth none." Second
              London 31 and <cite>Articles of Religion</cite> XIV agree.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 23:43">Luke 23:43</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 5:8">2 Corinthians 5:8</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:23">Philippians 1:23</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 9:27">Hebrews 9:27</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:7">1 John 1:7</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Millennial views (cross-cutting)</strong></td>
              <td>Not aligned to confessional families. Westminster and the Second
              London read amillennially or postmillennially; dispensational
              premillennialism spread among Baptists and Pentecostals after 1909;
              <cite>Statement of Fundamental Truths</cite> 13–14 is explicitly
              premillennial, with the rapture preceding the millennial reign.</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 20:1-6">Revelation 20:1–6</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 4:16-17">1 Thessalonians 4:16–17</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Zechariah 14:5">Zechariah 14:5</a>;
              <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 24:29-31">Matthew 24:29–31</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: REGISTER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="register">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Documents Themselves</h2>

        <p>A reference list of the texts cited above: what each is, when it was written,
        what it was written against, and whether it still binds anyone today.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Document</th>
              <th>Date</th>
              <th>Tradition and occasion</th>
              <th>Standing today</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Apostles' Creed</td>
              <td>2nd c. core; received text 8th c.</td>
              <td>Western baptismal creed, grown from the Old Roman Symbol</td>
              <td>Universal in the West; not used liturgically in the East</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Nicene Creed</td>
              <td>325; expanded 381</td>
              <td>Against Arianism; the third article expanded against denial of the
              Spirit's deity</td>
              <td>The one creed confessed nearly everywhere — apart from the filioque</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chalcedonian Definition</td>
              <td>451</td>
              <td>Against Eutyches and Nestorius: two natures, one person</td>
              <td>Binding for Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant churches; not received
              by the Oriental Orthodox</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Athanasian Creed</td>
              <td>c. 500</td>
              <td>Western; precise Trinitarian and christological formulation</td>
              <td>In the Lutheran and Anglican collections; unused in the East</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Canons of Orange</td>
              <td>529</td>
              <td>Against semi-Pelagianism; grace precedes even the will to believe</td>
              <td>Accepted across traditions; recovered in the 16th c. after medieval
              obscurity</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Schleitheim Articles</td>
              <td>1527</td>
              <td>Swiss Anabaptist; believers' baptism, the ban, separation, the sword,
              the oath</td>
              <td>Foundational for Anabaptist and Mennonite identity</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Augsburg Confession</td>
              <td>1530</td>
              <td>Lutheran; presented to Charles V as a claim to catholic continuity</td>
              <td>Binding on Lutheran churches worldwide</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Council of Trent</td>
              <td>1545–63</td>
              <td>Catholic; the answer to the Reformation, especially Session VI on
              justification</td>
              <td>Dogmatically binding; read alongside Vatican II</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Belgic Confession</td>
              <td>1561</td>
              <td>Reformed, by Guido de Brès, for persecuted churches in the Low
              Countries</td>
              <td>One of the Three Forms of Unity</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Heidelberg Catechism</td>
              <td>1563</td>
              <td>Reformed; commissioned by Elector Frederick III for teaching and
              preaching</td>
              <td>One of the Three Forms of Unity; the most pastoral of the confessions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Thirty-Nine Articles</td>
              <td>1563 / 1571</td>
              <td>Anglican; a settlement broad enough to hold one national church
              together</td>
              <td>Historic formulary across the Anglican Communion; subscription varies</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Second Helvetic Confession</td>
              <td>1566</td>
              <td>Reformed, by Heinrich Bullinger; the most widely adopted continental
              Reformed text</td>
              <td>Confessional in many European and Presbyterian bodies</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Formula / Book of Concord</td>
              <td>1577 / 1580</td>
              <td>Lutheran; settling intra-Lutheran disputes after Luther's death</td>
              <td>The Lutheran confessional corpus</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Canons of Dort</td>
              <td>1618–19</td>
              <td>Reformed; the answer to the Remonstrance of 1610</td>
              <td>One of the Three Forms of Unity</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dordrecht Confession</td>
              <td>1632</td>
              <td>Mennonite; eighteen articles including foot washing and the ban</td>
              <td>Confessional among conservative Mennonite bodies</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Westminster Standards</td>
              <td>1646–47</td>
              <td>Reformed; produced for the Church of England during the civil war,
              adopted in Scotland</td>
              <td>The Presbyterian standard worldwide</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Savoy Declaration</td>
              <td>1658</td>
              <td>Congregational; Westminster with the polity chapters rewritten</td>
              <td>Historic standard for Congregational churches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Confession of Dositheus</td>
              <td>1672</td>
              <td>Orthodox; the Synod of Jerusalem answering the Calvinising confession
              attributed to Cyril Lucaris</td>
              <td>Respected and widely cited; not a peer of the ecumenical councils</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Second London Confession</td>
              <td>1677 / 1689</td>
              <td>Particular Baptist; Westminster with baptism and the church rewritten</td>
              <td>The standard for Reformed Baptist churches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Articles of Religion</td>
              <td>1784</td>
              <td>Methodist; Wesley's abridgement of the Thirty-Nine Articles for
              American Methodists</td>
              <td>Doctrinal standard, with Wesley's Sermons and Notes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>New Hampshire Confession</td>
              <td>1833</td>
              <td>Baptist; a moderate-Calvinist statement for American congregations</td>
              <td>The direct ancestor of the Baptist Faith and Message</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chicago-Lambeth Quadrilateral</td>
              <td>1886 / 1888</td>
              <td>Anglican; four essentials proposed as a basis for reunion</td>
              <td>The standing Anglican ecumenical baseline</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Vatican I</td>
              <td>1870</td>
              <td>Catholic; <cite>Dei Filius</cite> on faith and reason,
              <cite>Pastor Aeternus</cite> on papal primacy and infallibility</td>
              <td>Dogmatically binding</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Statement of Fundamental Truths</td>
              <td>1916</td>
              <td>Pentecostal; sixteen articles, framed partly to answer the Oneness
              controversy</td>
              <td>Binding on Assemblies of God ministers; widely imitated</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Baptist Faith and Message</td>
              <td>1925, rev. 1963, 2000</td>
              <td>Southern Baptist; each revision responding to a live controversy</td>
              <td>The confessional standard of the Southern Baptist Convention</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Vatican II</td>
              <td>1962–65</td>
              <td>Catholic; <cite>Dei Verbum</cite> on revelation,
              <cite>Lumen Gentium</cite> on the church</td>
              <td>The governing framework for contemporary Catholic teaching</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Lausanne Covenant</td>
              <td>1974</td>
              <td>Trans-denominational evangelical; drafted for the Congress on World
              Evangelization</td>
              <td>A movement-wide basis of cooperation rather than a church confession</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chicago Statement on Biblical Inerrancy</td>
              <td>1978</td>
              <td>Trans-denominational evangelical; answering mid-century debates over
              Scripture</td>
              <td>Adopted by many institutions as a doctrinal basis</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Catechism of the Catholic Church</td>
              <td>1992, rev. 1997</td>
              <td>Catholic; a comprehensive post-conciliar reference</td>
              <td>The standard modern reference for Catholic teaching</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Joint Declaration on Justification</td>
              <td>1999</td>
              <td>Catholic and Lutheran, later joined by Methodist, Anglican and
              Reformed bodies</td>
              <td>An agreed statement, not a confession; scoped and with differences
              named</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Application</span>
          <p>Nothing in these tables settles which tradition is right, and they are not
          built to. What they are built to do is make the argument honest — to let you
          see that the positions you disagree with are held by serious people for
          scriptural reasons, that the confession you inherited was written by people
          under pressure answering a question you may not have known was asked, and
          that the ground you share with the believer across town is far larger than
          the ground you contest. Contend for the faith once delivered
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a>). Contend for it accurately, and
          test it as the Bereans did — including the parts you already agree with
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a>).</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Creeds of Christendom</cite>, Philip Schaff. 3 vols., 1877.
          <em>The standard reference collection — Greek, Latin and English texts of
          nearly every confession cited above, with historical introductions. Public
          domain and freely available.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Creeds and Confessions of Faith in the Christian Tradition</cite>,
          Jaroslav Pelikan and Valerie Hotchkiss, eds. Yale University Press, 2003.
          <em>The modern successor to Schaff: four volumes, wider coverage including
          African and Asian confessions, with Pelikan's historical volume separately.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Early Christian Doctrines</cite>, J. N. D. Kelly. A. and C. Black, 1958.
          <em>The standard account of how the patristic doctrines actually developed;
          fair, detailed, and not partisan.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Early Christian Creeds</cite>, J. N. D. Kelly. Longmans, 1950.
          <em>The definitive study of the rule of faith, the Old Roman Symbol, and the
          route from baptismal interrogation to conciliar creed.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Christian Tradition: A History of the Development of Doctrine</cite>,
          Jaroslav Pelikan. 5 vols., University of Chicago Press, 1971–89.
          <em>The most complete narrative account in English of how each tradition's
          doctrine came to be what it is.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Shape of Sola Scriptura</cite>, Keith Mathison. Canon Press, 2001.
          <em>Distinguishes the confessional Protestant position from individualism;
          the clearest account of how a Protestant can be both sola scriptura and
          confessional.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Orthodox Church</cite>, Timothy Ware (Kallistos). Penguin,
          1963 / 1993. <em>The standard introduction, and the best explanation of why
          the East has no confession of the Western kind.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Against Heresies</cite>, Irenaeus of Lyons. c. AD 180. Public domain.
          <em>Book I.10.1 is the earliest full statement of the rule of faith; Books
          III–IV develop the apostolic-succession argument.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Book of Concord</cite>, ed. Robert Kolb and Timothy Wengert.
          Fortress, 2000. <em>The Lutheran confessions in a modern critical translation,
          with the historical apparatus needed to read them in context.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification</cite>. Lutheran
          World Federation and the Catholic Church, 1999. <em>Short, freely available,
          and frequently described by people who have not read it. Read the annexe
          too.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

